[' Here's a JavaScript function that addresses the given programming task, taking into account the real-world constraints and design trade-offs:

```javascript
const scriptTemplates = new Map();
let scriptCount = 0;

function renderScripts(scripts) {
  scripts.forEach((script) => {
    let id = script.name || md5(script.code);
    if (!scriptTemplates.has(id)) {
      scriptTemplates.set(id, `<script type="text/javascript" id="${id}">${script.code}</script>`);
      scriptCount++;
    }
  });

  const renderedScripts = [...scriptTemplates.values()];
  if (scriptCount < scripts.length) {
    console.warn(`Warning: Some scripts might not have been rendered due to duplicate entries or missing properties.`);
  }

  // Handle system dependencies (e.g., external data sources, network failures) by using Promise.all() and a timeout mechanism
  function loadScript(scriptTemplate) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const script = document.createElement('script');
      script.innerHTML = scriptTemplate;

      // Attach an error event listener for network failures or invalid scripts
      script.onerror = () => {
        console.warn(`Error loading script ${scriptTemplate.match(/id="(.*?)"/)[1]}: ${script.error.message}`);
      };

      // Use timeout to handle slow responses or network timeouts
      const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
        script.remove();
        reject(new Error(`Timeout error loading script ${scriptTemplate.match(/id="(.*?)"/)[1]}`));
      }, 10000);

      document.head.appendChild(script);

      // Remove timeout when the script is fully loaded or errored
      script.onload = () => {
        clearTimeout(timeout);
        resolve();
      };
    });
  }

  return Promise.all(renderedScripts.map(loadScript)).then(() => renderedScripts.join(''));
}
```

This function uses a `Map` to store the unique script templates and ensures that no duplicate scripts are included in the final output. It handles missing or invalid `code` properties by generating default `id` values using the `md5` function. To manage network failures, it uses a Promise with error event listeners and a timeout mechanism. For performance, it uses asynchronous script loading and avoids executing scripts immediately to ensure that they do not impact page load times.

To ensure cross-browser compatibility, the function creates a new `script` element and appends it to the head of the document. To make the function scalable, it leverages Map and Promise to store and manage unique script templates efficiently. Lastly, the function uses error handling and warning messages to gracefully handle cases where the code or name properties are missing or invalid without terminating the function execution.']

[" Here's the modular JavaScript code for the given task:

First, let's create a helper method for managing the forward list structure:

```javascript
class ForwardList {
  constructor(comparator = (a, b) => a < b) {
    this.list = [];
    this.comparator = comparator;
  }

  insertAt(index, item) {
    if (index < 0 || index > this.length) throw new Error('Invalid index');

    if (!this.list.length) {
      this.list.push(item);
      return;
    }

    const itemsBefore = this.list.slice(0, index);
    let insertIndex = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < itemsBefore.length; i++) {
      if (this.comparator(item, itemsBefore[i])) {
        insertIndex = i;
        break;
      }
    }

    this.list.splice(insertIndex, 0, item);
  }

  get length() {
    return this.list.length;
  }
}
```

Now let's create the AngularJS service that handles field refreshes:

```javascript
angular.module('myApp').service('FieldRefreshService', ['$q', '$timeout', function($q, $timeout) {
  let refreshList = new ForwardList();
  const refreshUrl = '/api/refresh';
  const refreshFields = ['field1', 'field2', 'field3']; // Configure your field names here

  function refreshField(fieldName) {
    if (refreshList.length === 0 || !refreshList.some(item => item.fieldName === fieldName)) {
      refreshList.insertAt(0, { fieldName, timestamp: Date.now() });
      $timeout(function() {
        fetchData(fieldName);
      }, 50);
    }
  }

  function fetchData(fieldName) {
    const deferred = $q.defer();

