The code handles memory allocation and deallocation using standard library functions where appropriate, minimizing memory usage and ensuring thread safety by mapping the file into memory rather than loading it entirely into RAM. It also gracefully handles potential errors such as file I/O issues or invalid session IDs. The function identifies and logs any duplicate timestamps to maintain high performance and accuracy.

The design choices prioritize efficiency, usability, and maintainability by using a custom hashing function and an array-based data structure for the event log. However, the trade-off is that the configuration file needs to be managed separately and may require updates when adding new session ID ranges or modifying the hash function. Additionally, the code does not implement logging, validation, and storage functionalities mentioned in the task description, as those would depend on the specific requirements of the larger network event log framework.']

